      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4=3\\left(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)$\n- **II.** $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4=3\\left(e^{y}\\right)$\n- **III.** $x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4=3\\left(e^{x}\\right)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4 & =3\\left(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3\\left(\\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)-4}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{3 \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} - 4}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4 & =3\\left(e^{y}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3\\left(e^{y}\\right)-4}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3 e^{y} - 4}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4 & =3\\left(e^{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3\\left(e^{x}\\right)-4}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{3 e^{x} - 4}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
