  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166814,
      "id": "2fa0c4b2-8dc6-5823-a9bb-b933a9f9ee7f",
      "title": "Determining Whether an Ordinary Differential Equation is Autonomous or Nonautonomous"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e6f9f186-8e4d-5b4d-b28c-f9cf0637b83c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3=4\\left(\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)$\n- **II.** $x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3=4\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)$\n- **III.** $y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3=4\\left(e^{y}\\right)$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{\u2032}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3 & =4\\left(\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4\\left(\\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)-3}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{4 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)} - 3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3 & =4\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)-3}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{4 \\sqrt{x} - 3}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3 & =4\\left(e^{y}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4\\left(e^{y}\\right)-3}{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{4 e^{y} - 3}{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "50de27b1-5afd-5340-9c62-d6ff7259999e",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
